// onsa.toml
// [test]
// spec = ["§15.1", "§4.5", "§18.1"]

// S-103, S-713, S-719, S-721 (W4-04 U8b): a constant written in the position of a type argument, and a type
// written in the position of a const argument, are E0308 (§15.1: "定数を型引数の位置に書く、型を const 引数の
// 位置に書く"; §4.5: "const 引数は定数式"). A function written in the position of a type argument is E0308
// (a value is not a type) and in the position of a const argument it is E0417 (a value that is not a
// constant, §4.5). The same holds wherever the arguments are written: a parameter, a result, the right side
// of a type alias, a field, the type of a `const` and the annotation of a `let` in a body. The `::[…]` of an
// expression is not here: the form is not supported by this version (E0200), and a form that the version
// does not have is E0200 without reading the names in it (§15.1, §18.1).
// A const parameter is a value and a constant (§15.1), so it written in the position of a type is E0308
// too (R-282: a parameter of a function,
// an argument of `Option`, a field, a variant, a method of an `impl`, and in the body the annotation of a
// `let`, the target of an `as`, the types of an anonymous function). The constant of an `impl` written in the
// position of a type (`Self.N`, `P.N`) is E0308 (§4.4: `Self` is the alias of the target).
// The number of arguments is the E0412 of §4.5 and is not here. One error per unit (§18.1), so each form is
// in its own unit; the fine forms (a type in the position of a type argument, a constant in the position of
// a const argument, a const parameter as an argument or a length) are the fine units at the end of the file.

pub struct P {
  x: I32,
}

impl P {
  pub const N: U32 = 3
}

pub enum E {
  A,
  B(I32),
}

pub struct Wrap[T] {
  v: T,
}

pub struct Ring[T, const N: U32] {
  data: [T; N],
  head: U32,
}

pub struct Cells[const N: U32] {
  data: [F32; N],
}

type Al = P

const K: U32 = 4

fn g() -> U32 {
  1
}

pub flow lp(x: F32 at sample) -> F32 at sample {
  x
}

